---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout>
  <main class="min-h-screen bg-[#FAFAFA] flex flex-col">

    <!-- Navigation -->
    <nav id="navbar" class="fixed top-0 left-0 right-0 z-50 bg-[#FAFAFA] px-8 py-6 flex justify-between items-center transition-shadow duration-300">
      <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto w-full flex justify-between items-center">
        <a href="/" class="flex items-center gap-2 group">
          <div class="w-8 h-8 bg-[#2563EB] rounded-lg flex items-center justify-center group-hover:bg-[#F97316] transition-colors duration-300">
            <span class="font-mono text-white text-sm font-bold">SR</span>
          </div>
          <span class="font-mono text-[#1A1A2E] text-sm font-medium hidden md:block">sroberts.dev</span>
        </a>
        <div class="flex gap-8 text-sm font-medium text-[#6B7280]">
          <a href="/about" class="hover:text-[#1A1A2E] transition-colors">About</a>
          <a href="/projects" class="text-[#1A1A2E]">Projects</a>
          <a href="/blog" class="hover:text-[#1A1A2E] transition-colors">Blog</a>
          <a href="/#contact" class="hover:text-[#1A1A2E] transition-colors">Contact</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </nav>

    <div class="flex-grow">

      <!-- Back link + header -->
      <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-8 pt-40 pb-12">
        <a href="/projects/this-website" class="font-mono text-[#F97316] text-sm tracking-wider hover:underline">← Back to This Website</a>
        <p class="font-mono text-[#F97316] text-sm mt-6 mb-2 tracking-wider">Build Log</p>
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-extrabold text-[#1A1A2E] mb-6 leading-tight">
          Building the terminal widget
        </h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-[#6B7280] leading-relaxed">
          A draggable, resizable, persistent dummy terminal - with keyword
          matching, typo suggestions, and a self-built Cloudflare Worker
          for geolocation.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Article -->
      <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-8 pb-24 space-y-6 text-[#6B7280] leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          My inital idea was much more simple than what this project has developed into - a minimized chat-style bubble in the
          corner that expands into something more interesting than the standard chatbot. It ended up being one of the most involved features on the
          whole site, mostly because each addition sparked more intrest, and most importantly, I was enjoying testing it.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1A2E] pt-6">The core: a fake command line</h2>
        <p>
          At its simplest, the terminal is a text input and an output area,
          with a function that matches whatever you type against a list of
          known commands - <code class="bg-[#F3F4F6] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">whoami</code>,
          <code class="bg-[#F3F4F6] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">help</code>,
          <code class="bg-[#F3F4F6] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">about</code>,
          and so on.
          <code class="bg-[#F3F4F6] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">cd about</code> and
          <code class="bg-[#F3F4F6] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">cd projects</code> actually
          navigate the site - the terminal isn't just decorative, it's a real
          (if slightly silly) way to navigate, interact with and extract information from the site.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1A2E] pt-6">Making it feel less rigid</h2>
        <p>
          Exact commands only get you so far - i wanted to make the experience more natural to guests, allowing them to type more than just
          <code class="bg-[#F3F4F6] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm"> "contact"</code> and instead
          accepting phrases like "how do I get in touch?" So alongside the command
          list, there's a keyword-matching layer that catches natural
          phrasing and routes it to the same responses. It's not AI -
          the terminal makes a point of telling you itself if you ask - just pattern matching
          against a list of phrases. The honest framing felt natural as although I designed and am continueing to design the terminal to be robust
          any catch amny phrases, it's good to acknowlee there will be many inputs that will slip through the cracks.
        </p>
        <p>
          Typos get acknowleged too: a Levenshtein-distance check compares
          whatever you typed against the real command list and suggests the
          closest match, the same "did you mean?" trick that Git uses.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1A2E] pt-6">Drag, resize, and the state problem</h2>
        <p>
          Making it draggable meant learning the Pointer Events API - one set
          of event handlers that works for mouse, touch, and stylus input
          alike, rather than writing separate logic for desktop and mobile.
          Resizing works from all eight edges and corners, not just the
          browser-default bottom-right - each of the eight handles
          calculates a different combination of width, height, and position
          depending on which direction it's being dragged from.
        </p>
        <p>
          Mobile needed one extra fix: by default, a browser treats any
          finger-drag on a page as though the user is trying to scroll, which fought
          with dragging and resizing. Setting
          <code class="bg-[#F3F4F6] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm"> touch-action: none</code> on
          the drag handle and resize strips tells the browser to leave those
          specific areas alone.
        </p>
        <p>
          The trickiest part wasn't the dragging itself - it was making the
          terminal <em>remember</em> where it was. Astro builds a genuinely
          multi-page site, not a single-page app, so every navigation is a
          real page load with no memory of what happened on the last page.
          The fix was <code class="bg-[#F3F4F6] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm"> localStorage</code> -
          position, size, open/closed state, and conversation history all get
          written there on every change, and read back in on every page load.
          Open the terminal, drag it somewhere, cd to another
          page, and it reopens exactly where you left it.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1A2E] pt-6">A bug that only showed up after closing the tab</h2>
        <p>
          The original drag and resize code relied on
          <code class="bg-[#F3F4F6] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">setPointerCapture</code>,
          which locks pointer events to a specific element even if the
          cursor moves off it. It worked fine - until closing a tab and
          reopening it with Ctrl+Shift+T, at which point dragging
          stopped working whilst resizing and minimising remained fine.
        </p>
        <p>
          The fix was to stop listening on the small drag handle and resize
          strips altogether, and instead listen for
          <code class="bg-[#F3F4F6] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">pointermove</code> and
          <code class="bg-[#F3F4F6] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">pointerup</code> on
          <code class="bg-[#F3F4F6] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">document</code> instead
          - the standard, more resilient pattern for drag implementations.
          <code class="bg-[#F3F4F6] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">pointerdown</code> still
          starts on the specific handle, so you still have to grab the right
          spot to begin, but once a drag or resize starts, it keeps tracking
          the pointer regardless of capture, browser quirks, or how fast the
          cursor moves.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1A2E] pt-6">Building the geolocation command</h2>
        <p>
          <code class="bg-[#F3F4F6] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">location</code> shows
          country, region, city, timezone, and coordinates - but instead of
          calling a third-party geolocation API, it calls a small Cloudflare
          Worker written and deployed from scratch. Every request to this
          site already passes through Cloudflare's network, and Cloudflare
          already attaches location data to each request internally as part
          of how it routes traffic. The Worker just reads that data
          (<code class="bg-[#F3F4F6] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">request.cf</code>)
          and hands back a small JSON response - no external database, no
          API key, no raw IP address ever leaving Cloudflare's network.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1A2E] pt-6">A neofetch-style whoami</h2>
        <p>
          <code class="bg-[#F3F4F6] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">whoami</code> pulls
          real browser data - screen resolution, timezone, CPU core count,
          light/dark mode preference - directly from JavaScript's built-in
          <code class="bg-[#F3F4F6] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">navigator</code> object,
          nothing sent anywhere, nothing stored. It's formatted to look akin to
          <code class="bg-[#F3F4F6] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">neofetch</code>,
          a Linux cli tool that pairs a small ASCII logo with system info -
          even down to swapping in a different, appropriately-sized logo
          depending on how wide the current size of terminal.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1A2E] pt-6">Arrow-key history + a bug worth mentioning</h2>
        <p>
          Pressing ↑ and ↓ cycles back through previously typed commands, the
          same recall behaviouryou'll find on a regular shell - including restoring
          whatever you were mid-typing if you press ↓ back past the most
          recent command.
        </p>
        <p>
          That restore logic is also where a bug turned up: pressing
          ↓ while typing something new, without pressing ↑ first, wiped
          the text out entirely. The fix ended up being a single guard clause
          - if you're not actually browsing history, ↓ does nothing at all,
          rather than trying to "restore" a draft that wasn't captured. 
          Small bugwith a single-line fix, but the kind of thing
          that's only seemed obvious once you've actually used the feature rather
          than whilst building it.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1A2E] pt-6">What's next</h2>
        <p>
          Plan to add Pinch-to-zoom resizing for mobile next - tracking two
          simultaneous touch points instead of one, different logic from the single-pointer dragging thats curren=nrtly used. Longer
          term, I'd liike to pursue a bigger and better CTF section, I plan to make multiple scenarios- 
          or potentially one big overarching one, each new ctf buillding upon the last.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>

    <!-- Footer -->
    <footer class="bg-[#1A1A2E] border-t border-white/10 py-6">
      <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-8 flex justify-between items-center">
        <span class="font-mono text-[#6B7280] text-sm">© 2026 Scott Roberts</span>
        <span class="font-mono text-[#6B7280] text-sm">sroberts.dev</span>
      </div>
    </footer>
  </main>

  <script>
    const navbar = document.getElementById('navbar');
    window.addEventListener('scroll', () => {
      if (window.scrollY > 10) {
        navbar?.classList.add('shadow-md');
      } else {
        navbar?.classList.remove('shadow-md');
      }
    });
  </script>
</Layout>